Summarize raw sequence reads in FASTQ format, plain or compressed, from read counts and base quality to duplication and adapter content. For assembled contigs, see [Genomic Contig Summary](/docs/gui-usage/contig-summary/).

## Steps

1. Select `Genomics` in the navigation bar {% ref id="A" /%}.
2. Open `Summarize genomic reads` from the tool list on wide windows, or select the tool name {% ref id="B" /%} at the top of the page to switch tools.
3. Add the input files {% ref id="C" /%} with `Browse files`, `Browse folder`, or `Open archive`. SEGUI searches a folder and its subfolders for files that match the tool's input format. See [file input](/docs/gui-usage/intro/#file-input) for mobile options.
4. The `Input` tab of the file panel {% ref id="E" /%} lists the input files. On phones and narrow windows, open it with the files button next to the tool name. To remove files from the list, choose `Select`, pick the files, and select `Remove`. The files stay on your device.
5. Select the summary mode. For the Default and Complete modes, `Quality score offset` is `Auto` unless your reads use a known offset.
6. Select the run button {% ref id="D" /%}, labeled `Summarize`, to start the task.
7. In Complete mode, the `Workbench` tab of the file panel shows a `Report` card when the task finishes. Select `View report` to open the read report. You can also open `read-report.json` from the `Output` tab later.
8. Export or share the output (optional). See [exporting output](/docs/gui-usage/intro/#exporting-output).

## Summary Mode

The app supports the following summary modes:

- **Minimal**: read count only
- **Default**: generate essential statistics (see below)
- **Complete**: generate all the essential statistics plus statistics per read position, distributions, duplication, overrepresented sequences, and adapter content for each file.

## Essential statistics for raw reads

- Number of reads
- Number of bases
- Mean read length
- Minimum read length
- Maximum read length
- GC count
- GC content
- AT count
- AT content
- A, C, G, T, N count
- Low quality base count
- Mean base quality
- Min base quality
- Max base quality
- Median, standard deviation, and N50 of read length
- Q20 and Q30 bases
- Mean quality per read, and reads with mean quality below 20

## Read report

The read report shows the Complete mode statistics as interactive charts. Choose the sample at the top, and switch between tabs:

- **Overview**: statistics for every sample, with pass, warn, or fail checks for Q30 bases, mean quality, N content, duplication, adapters, poly-G, and overrepresented sequences. Sort by any column, and select a row to show that sample on the other tabs.
- **Per-base quality**: median and mean quality at each position over the good, reasonable, and poor quality zones.
- **Per-read quality**, **Read length**, and **N content**: compare several samples. Toggle samples with the chips above the chart.
- **GC content**: reads by GC content with the theoretical distribution.
- **Base composition**, **Duplication**, and **Adapters**: one sample at a time.
- **Overrepresented**: sequences above 0.1% of all reads, with a button to copy each one.

In Complete mode, `Duplication sketch size` sets how many distinct sequences are kept to estimate duplication across every read. Leave it empty for the default of 100,000. Larger values are more accurate and use more memory, and `0` counts every sequence exactly.

Hover over or touch a chart to see its values. Zoom along the horizontal axis with the mouse wheel or a pinch, and drag or scroll with a trackpad to pan.
